Sean's Port Knocker
-------------------

Because fwknop hasn't added ipv6 support yet...  This is very simple,
it doesn't have many features. It doesn't encode the client IP in the
port-knock packet (so is vulnerable to a MITM).  But really, all this
is meant to do is to stop the ssh port from being open to drive-by
login attempts. It's ssh itself that provides the real security.

Uses a 32 byte (256 bit) symmetric key to authenticate a valid knock.

Expects firewalld to handle the actual port opening and closing
after a timeout.

The knock port is hardcoded to be 9999.  But you can change the
"2209" below, to whatever port you want, as the one to be opened.

One thing to note, the client includes the time in the knock packet,
and the server rejects any packet which isn't within 2 secons of its
own time.  So client and server pretty much need to be syncing their
clocks to a time server.

PREPARE:

  To generate the common 256-bit, base64 encoded, systemtric key that
  you'll use on both the client and server:

    Use wireguard:
      wg genkey

    Or SSL:
      openssl rand -base64 32

SERVER INSTALL:

  Install the source files:
    put  seans_port_knocker.py into /usr/local/libexec/
    put  seans_port_knocker.service into /etc/systemd/system/
    put  spk.xml into /etc/firewalld/services/

  Open the port up to list for knocks (udp/999), change "FedoraServer" to real zone:
    firewall-cmd --reload
    firewall-cmd --permanent --zone=FedoraServer --add-service=spk
    firewall-cmd --reload

  Modify the service file so that it has the correct symetric key. You
  must run this on the server, since systemd encryption is tied to host:

    Paste the base64 encoded key into this command's stdin:
      systemd-creds encrypt --name=CHACHA20_KEY -p - - | xclip

    Then edit the /etc/systemd/system/seans_port_knocker.service,
    Paste the lines grabbed by xclip above into the [service] section,
    something like:

      SetCredentialEncrypted=CHACHA20_KEY: \
        Whxqht+dQJax1aZeCGLxmiAAAAABAAAADAAAABAAAADb5UMGXI2fjcjbchYAAAAAZrVij \
        YgTTh5+Qv0TEghczDanWRWZp3T5dUKpycfAMk5DoTZSf87TUSTM7wK8kzfWI23zUWrPX2 \
        3Ga+uuM6eR+69n/+4WUqG9MejdrzSZAhcjsoBmWqWemvvm+ROq

  Create a couple of firewall sets, that automatically expire entries:
      firewall-cmd --permanent --new-ipset=sshknock4 \
        --type=hash:ip --family=inet --option=timeout=30
      firewall-cmd --permanent --new-ipset=sshknock6 \
        --type=hash:ip --family=inet6 --option=timeout=30

  You can make sure they appear okay:
      firewall-cmd --reload
      nft list sets

  create a couple rich rules:
      firewall-cmd --add-rich-rule='rule family="ipv6" \
	source ipset=sshknock6 port port="2209" protocol="tcp" accept'
      firewall-cmd --add-rich-rule='rule family="ipv4" \
	source ipset=sshknock4 port port="2209" protocol="tcp" accept'

  You can make sure it's all configured with:
      firewall-cmd --list-all
      nft --set=sshknock6 --add-entry="2601::"
      nft list sets

  Enable the daemon:
      systemctl daemon-reload
      systemctl enable --now seans_port_knocker

  Make sure it started:
      systemctl status seans_port_knocker

CLIENT INSTALL:

  Create the Gnome Keyring entry to hold the symmetric base64 encoded key:

    Run:
      secret-tool store --label="Sean's Port Knocker" service seans-port-knocker username universal 

    Then paste the generated key into it, followed by ctrl-d ctrl-d:
      IEysXPIUNOmGrA23RNgrOWG4yuv1lCKwmWkIJd1cI3M=<ctrl-d><ctrl-d>

  You can use the Seahorse application to take a look that the entry has been saved:
    seahorse

  Install the source files:
    put  spk  into your user ~/bin/

  Test it
    nc <remote host> 2209     # will fail
    spk <remote host>
    nc <remote host> 2209     # sould show you the ssh login header

